WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.715
Good morning, it's Thursday, October 29.

00:00:02.782 --> 00:00:09.908
This is Signal in the AI Fog, your daily briefing on enterprise AI in production, written and read by AI.

00:00:09.976 --> 00:00:20.427
Today: OpenAI's GPT-6.1 Terra halves mid-tier prices, Mistral Large 4 weights still lack a licence, and Artificial Analysis index shifts to private tests.

00:00:20.495 --> 00:00:22.395
First, the story of the day.

00:00:22.463 --> 00:00:26.670
OpenAI released GPT-6.1 Terra on Monday, October twenty-sixth.

00:00:26.738 --> 00:00:29.656
It is the middle model of the GPT-6 family.

00:00:29.724 --> 00:00:39.497
It is rolling out to Plus, Pro, Enterprise and Edu users in ChatGPT Work and Codex, and developers can call it through the API as gpt-6.1-terra.

00:00:39.565 --> 00:00:44.654
OpenAI says standard API prices are fifty percent lower than GPT-5.6 Terra.

00:00:44.722 --> 00:00:55.580
It also says the model matches GPT-6 Astra on its Agents' Last Exam benchmark at one-tenth the price per token, and finishes tasks more than three times faster.

00:00:55.648 --> 00:01:06.846
Help Net Security lists the API price at one dollar per million input tokens, five cents per million cached input tokens, and five dollars per million output tokens.

00:01:06.914 --> 00:01:12.546
OpenAI says Terra meets the Critical cyber threshold in its Preparedness Framework.

00:01:12.614 --> 00:01:17.840
Its example of high-volume work is reading invoices into accounting software.

00:01:17.908 --> 00:01:20.690
The performance figures are OpenAI's own.

00:01:21.290 --> 00:01:26.727
The read here: last week's two headlines were both right, about different baselines.

00:01:26.792 --> 00:01:29.640
Fifty percent is against the previous Terra.

00:01:29.705 --> 00:01:31.453
One-tenth is against Astra.

00:01:31.517 --> 00:01:39.544
For routing, that makes Terra a serious candidate default for high-volume agent tasks, with a published rate card behind it.

00:01:39.608 --> 00:01:49.577
But the benchmark is OpenAI's, and the Critical cyber rating means your security team should read the safety documentation before you enable computer use.

00:01:49.642 --> 00:01:58.833
For a platform team: add the model to your gateway, shadow-test it on your own tasks, and compare cost per completed task, not cost per token.

00:01:58.898 --> 00:02:03.170
A cut like this will likely pressure other vendors' mid tiers too.

00:02:03.770 --> 00:02:05.593
Next, Sovereignty and Europe.

00:02:05.656 --> 00:02:17.979
ActuIA reports that on the evening of October twenty-eighth, Mistral Large 4's Hugging Face repository still showed an estimated weights date of October thirty-first, with no files and no licence.

00:02:18.042 --> 00:02:27.221
Mistral had said on October sixth that the weights, for a one trillion parameter model with 49 billion active, would come by the end of the month.

00:02:27.284 --> 00:02:37.280
Artificial Analysis lists its standard preview API price at one dollar thirty-six per million input tokens and four dollars eighteen per million output tokens.

00:02:37.880 --> 00:02:42.317
What this really signals: two days left, and still no licence.

00:02:42.388 --> 00:02:45.537
Treat Saturday as a possibility, not a plan.

00:02:45.609 --> 00:02:50.976
Legal and engineering should read the licence together, the day it appears.

00:02:51.047 --> 00:03:01.710
Until then, the API price is your baseline for any self-hosting comparison, and a model this size needs serious GPU memory, so run those numbers now.

00:03:02.310 --> 00:03:03.265
Next, Platform.

00:03:03.329 --> 00:03:11.350
Fox News' live blog reports that Amazon is telling its software engineers to stop tokenmaxxing and to use AI more judiciously.

00:03:11.950 --> 00:03:16.846
The bigger picture: token consumption has become a management topic.

00:03:16.918 --> 00:03:21.383
Pushing usage up drives adoption, then someone reads the bill.

00:03:21.455 --> 00:03:24.767
Treat this as a signal, given the thin detail.

00:03:24.839 --> 00:03:32.040
For a platform team, per-team quotas and cost per outcome at the gateway turn that debate into data.

00:03:32.640 --> 00:03:33.532
Next, Quality.

00:03:33.596 --> 00:03:38.694
ActuIA reports that Artificial Analysis has published version five of its index.

00:03:38.758 --> 00:03:49.018
Sixty percent of it now rests on tests whose questions or answers stay secret, after an earlier increase aimed at stopping models being tuned to the leaderboard.

00:03:49.082 --> 00:03:52.778
Open-weight models lose between twelve and sixteen points.

00:03:52.842 --> 00:03:58.768
Mistral Large 4 falls from thirty-eight to twenty-five, below Claude Haiku 5.5 at thirty-six.

00:03:58.832 --> 00:04:01.190
Claude Opus 5.5 stays at fifty-eight.

00:04:01.790 --> 00:04:08.822
Reading between the lines: moving to private tests is sensible, but it shows how unstable rankings are.

00:04:08.890 --> 00:04:12.099
The models did not change, and the scores fell.

00:04:12.167 --> 00:04:15.581
Never base a routing decision on one public index.

00:04:15.649 --> 00:04:21.930
Your own private business evaluation set is the only benchmark that does not move under you.

00:04:22.530 --> 00:04:24.091
Next, Retail and agents.

00:04:24.156 --> 00:04:35.343
Walmart announced on October twenty-first that Agent Profiles will launch within a month, with a beta Agent Gateway and terms for agents shopping at Walmart and Sam's Club.

00:04:35.408 --> 00:04:39.441
Today, agent purchases sit mixed with a customer's own orders.

00:04:39.506 --> 00:04:48.677
Profiles will let customers approve purchases, set spending limits and see which were made by an agent, says Walmart executive Daniel Danker.

00:04:48.742 --> 00:04:53.880
Digital Commerce 360 says Walmart has not named the supported agents at launch.

00:04:54.480 --> 00:04:58.216
Why it matters: the useful lesson is the separation.

00:04:58.288 --> 00:05:04.036
Agent identity, spending limits and purchase records become first-class objects.

00:05:04.108 --> 00:05:12.659
For a retailer, that frames your own design question: how do you identify an agent, cap it, and report on its activity?

00:05:12.730 --> 00:05:15.820
Watch which agents are supported at launch.

00:05:16.420 --> 00:05:17.168
A follow-up.

00:05:17.231 --> 00:05:23.465
Mistral Large 4 weights were promised by the end of October, and the placeholder points to Saturday.

00:05:23.527 --> 00:05:29.949
The licence file is still missing from the repository, so the open-weight release has not happened yet.

00:05:30.012 --> 00:05:31.383
The number of the day.

00:05:31.445 --> 00:05:32.443
Thirteen points.

00:05:32.505 --> 00:05:41.359
That is how far Mistral Large 4 falls on the new Artificial Analysis index, from thirty-eight to twenty-five, with the model itself unchanged.

00:05:41.421 --> 00:05:46.845
Sixty percent of the index now rests on secret tests, so keep a private evaluation set.

00:05:46.907 --> 00:05:47.780
What to watch.

00:05:47.843 --> 00:05:56.509
Mistral Large 4 open weights: the Hugging Face placeholder says Saturday, October thirty-first, and the licence file is what to read first.

00:05:56.571 --> 00:06:00.998
AWS has still not published findings from its sovereign cloud exercise.

00:06:01.060 --> 00:06:05.549
Walmart's Agent Profiles are due within a month of October twenty-first.

00:06:05.611 --> 00:06:17.769
Further out: the EuroHPC gigafactory bid deadline on November twelfth, Microsoft Ignite from November seventeenth, Argon's price doubling on December first, and AI Act marking on December second.

00:06:17.831 --> 00:06:19.639
And a question for your team.

00:06:19.702 --> 00:06:28.742
Which of your mid-tier routes could move to GPT-6.1 Terra, and do you have a private evaluation set that would prove it before any traffic moves?

00:06:28.804 --> 00:06:30.051
That's it for today.

00:06:30.114 --> 00:06:32.545
Sources are in the episode description.

00:06:32.607 --> 00:06:34.478
This was Signal in the AI Fog.

00:06:34.540 --> 00:06:35.600
See you tomorrow.
